WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.259
Listen, no matter what you do in life, you're
always negotiating.

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:08.980
Whether you're dealing with your boyfriend,
girlfriend, husband, wife, kids, boss, parents,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.000
family, cousins.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
It doesn't matter.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.599
You're always negotiating, so the sooner you
become a better negotiator, and learn the

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:19.460
mistakes you're making, the better results
you'll start getting when it comes down to

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.460
negotiating.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.160
Today, I'm going to talk to you about the
14 common mistakes people make when it comes

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.160
down to negotiating.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.400
Now there's a lot more out there.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.840
I'm just giving you 14 of them.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.220
Number one, letting your emotions get in the
way when you're negotiating.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.910
Let me explain what I mean by this.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.630
It doesn't matter what it is, if a parent
goes to school and is talking to a principal

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:44.170
because their kid did something wrong, the
parent always thinks their kids are perfect,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.560
and they don't make mistakes, because it's
their product.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.460
The same when you're selling a house to somebody
else, you always think your house is worth

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.110
more than it really is because it's your house.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.739
You've been living in it for eight years,
nine years.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:01.820
The market would generally tell you what your
house is worth, what you car is worth, whatever

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:02.840
you're selling.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.840
You got to figure out a way to put yourself
out of the situation and kind of be a consultant

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.200
looking in, instead of being somebody that's
involved emotionally in it where you don't

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.100
typically end up negotiating the proper way.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.600
You can't just let your emotions get into
... Especially if the other person says, "I

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.740
think this thing's only worth $4,000."

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.650
You think it's $10,000, you're absolutely
furious.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.850
You may lose a deal there, and you got to
kind of figure out a way what it is.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.759
Number two, misinterpretation of position.

00:01:27.759 --> 00:01:32.819
This if very important, because sometimes
you're talking to somebody, and they say something,

00:01:32.819 --> 00:01:34.529
and you get upset.

00:01:34.529 --> 00:01:37.569
They didn't mean what you thought they meant.

00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:42.240
Instead of getting upset, ask two, or three,
or four more questions, probing questions

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.649
to ask them what they mean by this.

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:45.649
"Do you mean this?

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:46.649
Do you mean this?"

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:47.649
"That's not at all what I'm saying.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:49.180
I'm saying this because of this."

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.990
"Oh, I didn't even think about that."

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.029
"Okay, no problem."

00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:57.039
A lot of times, deals don't go through because
there's not enough questions being asked,

00:01:57.039 --> 00:02:00.509
because the two parties negotiating misinterpreted
each other.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:03.319
All right, so you you got to make sure you
got a lot more questions there so this doesn't

00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:04.319
happen.

00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:07.939
Number three, research, research, research.

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:09.399
Do as much research as you can.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:10.539
Let me give you a story here.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.250
We had a case where somebody contacted us.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:17.400
They wanted me to be in this documentary,
and "Oh, this documentary is going to have

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.970
a Buddha in it, and Tony Rob ..." All this
stuff.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.230
It sounded so fancy, and we want you to be
an associate producer, and we're standing

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.090
there saying, "Wow this is pretty exciting.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.209
This is something that maybe we'll entertain,
want to be a part of," until we found out

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:37.180
that the executive producer that was a part
of it had, had a major, major scandal, New

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:43.340
York Times type of scandal, that he was dealing
with, from a relationship with a younger girl.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.590
All this other stuff.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.120
The moment we found out, we backed down.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.569
We said, "We're not even doing any business
with this."

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:48.580
We almost did.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.560
We almost did because we didn't do research.

